[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106105,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Extracardiac rhabdomyomas are benign mesenchymal tumours with skeletal muscle differentiation. Fetal rhabdomyoma accounts for the majority of paediatric cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106106,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8903/0 Fetal rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n<p>8904/0 Adult rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n<p>8905/0 Genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106107,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E86.2 &amp; XH8WG9 Rhabdomyoma &amp; Rhabdomyoma, NOS</p>\r\n<p>2E86.2 &amp; XH4729 Rhabdomyoma &amp; Fetal rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n<p>2E86.2 &amp; XH5AF2 Rhabdomyoma &amp; Genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106108,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> extracardiac rhabdomyoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106109,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetal rhabdomyoma; adult rhabdomyoma; genital rhabdomyoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106110,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The vast majority of cases arise in the head and the neck, particularly the posterior auricular region. Other sites include the chest, mediastinum, abdomen, pelvis, extremities, epididymis {22520028; 11035587}, vulva {26352088}, tonsil {26246927}, and scrotum {23768526}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106111,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A growing mass may cause proptosis, decreased vision, airway obstruction, or hoarseness {8319954}. See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/44/174/18078\" target=\"_blank\">#18078</a>Table&nbsp;6.06 for a description of the subtypes listed above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106112,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetal rhabdomyomas occur at all ages, with two peaks (&lt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;years and 60&nbsp;years) {26214666}. About 25% of cases are congenital, and these account for 2% of all striated muscle tumours {8319954}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106113,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetal rhabdomyoma may be associated with naevoid basal cell carcinoma (Gorlin) syndrome, caused by loss-of-function mutations in the tumour suppressor gene <em>PTCH1</em> {23780909}. Several cases have been reported with Birt&ndash;Hogg&ndash;Dub&eacute; syndrome {29744825}, caused by <em>FLCN</em> mutation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106114,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Dysregulation of hedgehog signalling is an important genetic underpinning in the pathogenesis of both syndromic and sporadic rhabdomyomas {28700439; 16294371}. In syndromic forms, hedgehog signalling is activated by the loss of the inhibitory function of <em>PTCH1</em>. In the other forms, the mechanisms are unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106115,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetal rhabdomyomas are well-circumscribed masses with glistening cut surfaces. Multicentric tumours have been reported in association with naevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome {24517962}. Sessile or pedunculated polyps occur on mucosal surfaces. Tumour sizes range from 10&nbsp;to 130&nbsp;mm (median: 30&nbsp;mm) {8319954}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106116,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The classic fetal (myxoid) type contains primitive spindle cells with indistinct cytoplasm arranged in irregular bundles within a myxoid background, interspersed with immature skeletal muscle fibres that resemble fetal myotubes. The intermediate fetal type (juvenile or cellular) shows greater cellularity, less myxoid stroma, and a wide spectrum of skeletal muscle differentiation including differentiated rhabdomyoblasts or ganglion-like rhabdomyoblasts. Mitoses can be relatively frequent (as many as 5&nbsp;mitoses per 50&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup>), but tumours lack substantial nuclear atypia, infiltrative margins, atypical mitoses, and necrosis {8319954; 19165887}.</p>\r\n<p>Tumour cells are positive for desmin, MSA, focal SMA, and occasional S100. Myogenin and MYOD1 are positive in primitive spindle cells and rhabdomyoblasts.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106117,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106118,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fetal rhabdomyoma occurs in association with inherited or sporadic inactivating germline mutations in <em>PTCH1</em> {24517962}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106119,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a well-circumscribed mass with mature rhabdomyoblasts lacking atypia or necrosis; mitotic activity may be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106120,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 106121,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Complete excision of fetal rhabdomyoma is curative, with exceptional recurrences {19165887; 16406074}. Rare cases of possible malignant transformation have been reported.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:54:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]